  "question": {
    "text": "Consider that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;metal ion&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mfenced></math>&nbsp;forms a complex with aqua ligands, and the spin only magnetic moment of the complex is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>.90</mo><mtext>&#8201;</mtext><mi>BM</mi></math>. The geometry and the crystal field stabilization energy of the complex is :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>&#160;octahedral and&#160;</mtext><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>P</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "tetrahedral and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8722;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.6</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#916;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>&#160;octahedral and&#160;</mtext><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>&#160;tetrahedral and&#160;</mtext><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>P</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Since spin only magnetic moment is 4.90 BM so number of unpaired electrons must be 4.&nbsp;There are two possibilities:</p><p>(1) If the complex is octahedral, then it has to be high spin complex with configuration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo></math> in that case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CFSE</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math><br />(2) If the complex is tetrahedral then its electronic configuration will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><msup><mmultiscripts><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow><none></none><mprescripts></mprescripts><none></none><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mmultiscripts><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CFSE</mi></math> will <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>be</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>X</mi><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>t</mi></math></p>"
  }
}